# Which status-check contexts the `main` branch ruleset requires, and every job that reports a
# context it does NOT require. Held by `cargo xtask check-workflows`.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Which contexts gate a merge lived only in GitHub's API, so *the four cross
# link legs block a merge* was believed by readers, written down nowhere, and checked by nothing.
# Read on 2026-09-05 from `repos/telekom/sutura/rulesets/21290172` ("main", target `branch`,
# enforcement `active`):
#
#     required_status_checks: [ { context: "ci" } ]
#     strict_required_status_checks_policy: false
#
# Exactly one context. Classic branch protection is absent as well - `branches/main/protection`
# answers `404 Branch not protected` - and the second repository ruleset ("main: human review", id
# 22184737) is `disabled` and declares no rule at all. The ruleset's other rules are `deletion`,
# `non_fast_forward`, `required_linear_history`, `merge_queue` and `pull_request`.
#
# READ ON A DATE, AND ONLY THAT DATE. A red link leg DOES NOT BLOCK A MERGE as read on 2026-09-05.
# Whether one ever did is not in any response above: a ruleset's history is not in its
# representation, and the legs reported different context strings before they were renamed, so
# whether some earlier ruleset required one of those earlier names is exactly what this record
# cannot say. The earlier wording here was *has never blocked a merge*, which is a claim about the
# past that nothing read - reported in review, and it is the standard the rest of this file keeps.
#
# Confirmed live rather than inferred: on a pull request whose `ci` job had gone green while all
# four `cross / link (<triple>)` legs were still pending, `gh pr view --json mergeStateStatus` read
# `UNSTABLE` - GitHub's word for *the required checks are satisfied, something non-required is not*
# - and not `BLOCKED`, which is what the same command read while `ci` itself was pending.
#
# WHAT THE MERGE QUEUE TAKES ITS SIGNAL FROM IS NOT MEASURED HERE. The `merge_queue` rule is in the
# list above, and its own parameters are the queue's build and merge sizes, its grouping strategy,
# its merge method and a check-response timeout - no status-check list of its own. So *the queue
# uses the same single required context* is GitHub's documented behaviour rather than something
# this read establishes, and no queue run is cited. Stated as unmeasured rather than asserted.
#
# THE LIMIT, and it is why this is a declaration rather than a proof: the API is the authority and
# it is unreachable from the sandbox the gate runs in. **What is no longer a limit is the
# organisation level.** `repos/telekom/sutura/rules/branches/main` returns the EFFECTIVE rules for
# the branch - every ruleset that applies, whatever its level - and needs only repository read.
# Measured 2026-09-05: six rules, every one of them `ruleset_source_type: Repository` from ruleset
# 21290172, required status checks exactly one context, and no organisation-sourced rule
# contributing anything. So for THIS branch an organisation ruleset cannot be invisible. What
# remains unknown is narrower and is not about `main`: an organisation ruleset that does not apply
# to this branch today - one targeting other branch patterns, or set to a different enforcement -
# is outside that response, and reading the organisation's own ruleset list still needs `admin:org`.
#
# WHAT THE GATE HOLDS:
#
#   * every `[required]` context resolves to a job THAT REPORTS IT - by its `name:` where it has
#     one, otherwise by its id. A required context that no longer REPORTS is a permanently
#     pending merge, which is worse than an ungated leg, so a rename fails a gate here instead of
#     the merge box later. A matrix leg and a reusable-workflow call report a context no `jobs:`
#     key spells, so such an entry is REFUSED with that sentence rather than reported as a context
#     nothing reports - which is what it used to print, falsely, on the one remedy this file
#     discusses. A job in a CALLED workflow is read for the rule below and CANNOT resolve an entry
#     here: it reports `<caller job> / <job> (<value>)`, so `link` in this section is a context
#     nothing sends. Reading those jobs without carrying that distinction made the gate answer
#     *this context reports* about a string GitHub never emits - green over a permanently pending
#     merge, and the reason a job carries whether its context is the whole string;
#   * every job in a workflow that can report on a pull request is in one of the two sections
#     below - INCLUDING a job in a workflow that a gating one CALLS. Its own `on:` is
#     `workflow_call`, so the event test alone read none of its jobs while its legs reported on
#     every pull request, and the four link legs were classified by nothing while this gate
#     printed *every gating job classified*. A new job is then a decision - required, or advisory -
#     rather than an omission, wherever it is written;
#   * every `[advisory]` entry names a job that exists, so an entry cannot outlive its job.
#
# WHAT IT CANNOT HOLD: that this file matches the ruleset. The API is the authority and it is
# unreachable from the sandbox the gate runs in - the same limit `cargo xtask check-venues` states
# about its own invocation rule. Re-read the ruleset when it changes, and move the date above.
#
# An `[advisory]` entry is `<workflow file>:<job id>`, because the id is the identity a workflow
# rename does not move. A `[required]` entry is the CONTEXT STRING, because that is what the
# ruleset holds.

[required]

# The one context the ruleset requires. Everything routed through this job IS gated: the structural
# gates, the lints, the tests, the doctests, the causality gate, the API pages. A STEP inside it is
# gated for the same reason, which is how a gate that only runs there is genuinely gating.
ci

# The category aggregator: a plain job, so its context resolves like any other - the reason the file
# header prefers one over a matrix-expanded or called-workflow context. It holds that a category-
# gated leg may be skipped green only when the diff did not select its category, so a filter that
# silently skips an affected adapter fails here rather than publishing a green-but-red merge. This
# entry and the `ci` entry beside it are the two contexts the ruleset's `required_status_checks`
# holds - the second is the owner-held edit `ci-aggregate` needs in the same ruleset to actually
# block a merge.
ci-aggregate

[advisory]

# A red leg in this section does not block a merge, read on 2026-09-05, in the queue or out of it,
# with no override and no bypass - nobody has to click anything. Present tense on purpose: see the
# dated paragraph in the header for what a ruleset's representation does and does not say about
# its own past. That is the status quo made visible, not a recommendation: whether any of these
# should become required is a decision, and the cost of requiring one is four context names to
# maintain by hand - which `cargo xtask check-workflows` REFUSES, because it derives a plain job's
# context and cannot spell `cross / link (<triple>)` - or one aggregating job to add, whose plain
# context it can check like any other.

# The cross link matrix. It calls `cross-link.yml`, so its legs report `cross / link (<triple>)`
# and the required set contains none of them. The risk the job exists for - the allocator's C for
# the two musl triples - is therefore covered advisorily: a pull request that breaks the cross link
# for a published triple can merge with the leg red.
ci.yml:cross

# The called workflow's own job - the one that reports those four legs. ADVISORY DELIBERATELY, and
# this is the entry where that decision is written down rather than left to the reader:
#
#   * REQUIRING THE LEGS DIRECTLY was weighed and refused. Four context strings maintained by
#     hand, each carrying a matrix value and the caller's job name, and `cargo xtask
#     check-workflows` cannot derive any of them - so the gate would be blind exactly where the
#     cost is. Note what does NOT discriminate here, because the first draft of this bullet leaned
#     on it: *a required context that stops reporting is a permanently pending merge* is equally
#     true of `ci`, which is required. What decides it is only that these contexts are
#     matrix-expanded.
#   * MOVING THE CHECK INSIDE `ci` would make it required, since `ci` is the required context and a
#     STEP inside it is gated. It is refused on what the matrix buys: four runners build four
#     dependency closures in parallel, so a branch waits for one target instead of four. Inside
#     `ci` the same work is sequential, on the job every pull request waits for, and `needs: [ci]`
#     exists so a tree that fails clippy does not spend four runners proving it also links.
#   * MAKING `ci` DEPEND ON THE LEGS is not available: `cross` has `needs: [ci]`, so that edge
#     cannot be inverted without a cycle.
#   * AN AGGREGATING JOB IS AVAILABLE, AND WAS NOT TAKEN. This is the option the previous wording
#     folded into the line above and so declared closed - which was wrong twice over, because it is
#     also the remedy `cargo xtask check-workflows` PRINTS when a `[required]` entry names a
#     context it cannot derive: *require an aggregating job instead, its context resolves like any
#     other*. A job with `needs: [cross]` reporting one plain context, plus one ruleset edit, would
#     make this coverage required AND checkable by the rule above - a rename of the legs behind it
#     would then fail that rule instead of the merge box. It costs one hand-maintained name and one
#     edit nobody here can verify, and it was not taken; that is a decision, not an impossibility,
#     and the reader is entitled to know which. What *the ruleset is unreachable from the sandbox*
#     is, is a limit on VERIFICATION rather than a reason: `ci` is required by exactly the same
#     unverifiable edit.
#
# So the coverage stays advisory, and what is mechanised instead is that the classification is
# complete: this entry is what `cargo xtask check-workflows` needs in order to fail when a job is
# added to the called workflow, which is the drift that was invisible before.
cross-link.yml:link

# Posts the CRAP delta as a pull-request comment. Advisory by design: it reports, and `check-crap`
# inside `ci` is what gates the policy.
ci.yml:crap-comment

# Publishes this pull request's own realised store paths to the additive PR cache. Advisory by
# design: it warms the next push of the same PR, so a red or missing publish costs a rebuild and
# never blocks a merge - a fork's pull request cannot see its environment either, which alone
# would leave it permanently pending.
ci.yml:pr-cache

# The live BigQuery acceptance venue. Advisory deliberately rather than by omission: it needs an
# environment a fork's pull request cannot see, so requiring it would leave every fork pull request
# permanently pending - the failure mode this file's own header warns about.
ci.yml:bigquery-acceptance

# The documentation site. `verify` proves the site builds under `--strict` on a pull request;
# `publish` runs on everything else and reports no context on one. Neither is required, and
# `check-docs` inside `ci` is what holds the nav and the pages.
docs.yml:verify
docs.yml:publish

# The supply-chain audit. It runs on a pull request that touches a manifest or a lock, on a push to
# `main`, and weekly regardless - and the weekly run is the point of the file, since most
# advisories arrive against a dependency already in the lock. The pull-request leg is a path-scoped
# duplicate of the `nix run .#deny` step inside `ci`, which IS required, so nothing here is the only
# reader of an advisory. Found by this gate on its first run, which is what the gate is for: nobody
# had classified it, and `ci.yml`'s own trigger comment already says that making this required
# would need a `merge_group` trigger added here.
security-audit.yml:audit

# The runner probe is DISPATCH-ONLY and reports no pull-request context, so it is deliberately
# absent here - a measurement tool, run by hand when a runner needs checking, never a reportable on
# a gating event. `.github/workflows/runner-probe.yml` carries the design.

# Generated input against the parsers that read untrusted input is DELIBERATELY ABSENT here, the
# way the runner probe above is, and the sentence that used to stand here classified both its jobs
# `[advisory]` over no entry at all. `.github/workflows/fuzz.yml` triggers on the release tag and
# on `workflow_dispatch` - neither of them a gating event - so it reports no status-check context
# and there is nothing for this record to classify. It argues at length why a time-budgeted search
# over random input must gate neither a merge nor a release. What IS gated on every pull request is
# `check-fuzz` inside the hygiene sweep, which reads the harness and costs milliseconds.
